{
  "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
  "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES: DALE CENDALI, New York, New York; on behalf of the Petitioners. MICHAEL B. KIMBERLY, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lucky Brand Dungarees, Inc.",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. CENDALI: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: This Court should reverse the Second Circuit because it erred in holding that a defense never previously litigated to judgment can be barred in a case involving new claims."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lucky Brand Dungarees, Inc.",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "To be clear, we are not arguing that defenses may never be barred under existing law. Issue preclusion could bar a previously resolved defense. And previously unresolved defenses cannot be raised in the context of a judgment enforcement action or as a claim in an action collaterally attacking a prior judgment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Before we get to that, Ms. Cendali, could you explain why you abandoned the release defense in the first action? You did raise it, and then you dropped it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Counsel, you raise a point about the lack of symmetry here that would be created. I suppose we could remedy that, couldn't we, and say that if a plaintiff had a claim in time 2 that was available, similar to the one in time 1, just as here, and could have brought a cause of action but forgot to do so in time 1, it should be barred from doing so in time 2. Would that -- would that solve the asymmetry problem?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Could they --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Can you tell me what the theory is, what you think the 2005 settlement -- or 2003 settlement agreement means?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lucky",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It -- it doesn't mean that. What it means is what the district court held it to mean. If -- it's a nice summary of it in its decision granting our motion to dismiss, which led to the appeal in Marcel II. And what it means is that in exchange for $650,000, my client, Lucky, agreed not to use \"Get Lucky\" anymore, but that for any trademarks that it had registered or used prior to the date of the settlement agreement, which would include \"Lucky Brand,\" the name of our store, and other kinds of things like -- other enumerated things, any trademarks that used the word \"Lucky\" prior to that date, all future claims would be extinguished."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lucky",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So the settlement agreement doesn't -- it's not an offensive document. They can continue to use their sole registered trademark, \"Get Lucky,\" to their hearts' content."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: You said before a judgment enforcement action and a collateral attack on a judgment. Do you view those as different things?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Kimberly",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the failure to raise the defense in one of those prior actions can bar the raising of a defense in the later actions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Kimberly",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would start with City of Beloit. This is a case from 1968. It predates the Davis case, on which my friend on the other side relies, and it -- it stands for exactly this proposition."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Kimberly",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the only permanent injunction in this case was the permanent injunction that was entered into interlocutorily, one year before the trial in this case. It was the injunction entered as a sanction because Lucky had misrepresented to the court in Marcel that it was no longer using \"Get Lucky.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, how -- how does this undermine the prior judgment?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2019-18-1086",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "11b7eb020fd6053d75e88a358f616f64886177670403973c907aa95eeb78d4ef",
      "evidence_id": "ev-11b7eb020fd6-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted."
    },
